        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"rostand-cyrano-de-bergerac-ff037eb9b728bd04\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":2,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.88,\"analysis\":\"Roxane directly avows affectionate love or fondness for Cyrano. The immediate repetition after his promise, the initial “Oh!”, and the accompanying blown kiss give the line some independent force as an affectionate emotional effusion, though it remains primarily a state-presenting avowal. It does not undertake a relational commitment.\",\"ambiguity\":\"French “je vous aime” can suggest romantic love, but Roxane’s references to tender friendship and her declared love for Christian strongly favor affectionate, nonromantic fondness here. The degree of expressive/reflexive force is less certain than the strong T force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Roxane directly says “Oh ! je vous aime !” to Cyrano while leaving, accompanied by a blown kiss.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Roxane has just obtained Cyrano’s sworn promise to protect Christian, whom she romantically loves. Her line expresses warm gratitude and established tender friendship toward Cyrano, not a romantic declaration or promise of commitment. Its light, immediate placement and accompanying kiss support some expressive effusion; Cyrano’s subdued replies and subsequent immobility create dramatic irony because he loves her romantically, but that irony does not alter her utterance’s core force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"After Cyrano promises to be Christian’s friend and prevent duels, Roxane responds: “Oh ! je vous aime bien.”\",\"supports\":\"The utterance is an affectionate response to Cyrano’s helpful promise and primarily presents Roxane’s fondness as true.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Roxane then sends Cyrano “un petit baiser de la main” and says: “Oh ! je vous aime !”\",\"supports\":\"The affectionate gesture, interjection, and immediate reiteration support some expressive or effusive force in addition to the avowal.\",\"confidence\":0.9},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Roxane has explicitly said of Christian, “Enfin, je l’aime,” asks Cyrano to tell Christian to write, and subsequently tells Cyrano, “Nous sommes / De grands amis !”\",\"supports\":\"This contrast identifies Christian as the romantic object and frames her love for Cyrano as tender friendship rather than courtship or a new relational undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"After Roxane exits, Cyrano remains motionless with lowered eyes and later silences Le Bret’s question about her.\",\"supports\":\"The scene creates dramatic asymmetry between Roxane’s friendly affection and Cyrano’s painful romantic investment; this is contextual irony rather than a distinct core force in her words.\",\"confidence\":0.94}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the avowal of affectionate fondness, while E captures its limited additional character as an immediate affectionate effusion. P is unnecessary, and the dramatic irony and gratitude belong to context rather than requiring O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
